# ── THE COMPILED ARTIFACT IS NOT COMMITTED, AND THE REASON IS A REPO CONTRACT ──
#
# scripts/check-artifact-reproducibility.mjs governs every committed
# `*/dist/*.wasm`: it must appear in scripts/artifact-provenance.json with the
# toolchain that produced it, because "a committed artifact with no recorded
# toolchain cannot be audited". This lane cannot write that ledger — it is held
# under an active review-state claim by modules-shipped-artifact-resign-wave
# (measured: `graphctl claim --paths modules:scripts/artifact-provenance.json`
# is refused as a cross-claim conflict), and committing a binary that breaks a
# contract whose whole purpose is auditing binaries is the wrong half of the
# trade.
#
# Nothing is lost that was not already build-first here. Both flow test suites
# already read data-source/terrain-source/dist/isomorphic/module.wasm, which
# this lane has always gitignored, and tools/terrain-pyramid/run.mjs already
# refuses with "build the flow first" if the runtime is absent. `npm run build`
# in this directory produces it. What IS committed is the composition record the
# build writes beside it — dist/artifact.json (node ids, dispatch model,
# capabilities and every dependency's sha256), dist/flow.json, dist/flow.plg and
# dist/plugin-manifest.json — so what the flow links, and what it is allowed to
# do, stays auditable in git without an unledgered binary.
#
# The exact sha256 of the artifact a given run executed is recorded in
# tools/terrain-pyramid/evidence/<run>/REPRODUCE.md. Graph follow-up to put
# these four artifacts back under the ledger once that claim clears:
# modules-terrain-artifact-provenance-ledger.
dist/*.wasm
dist/isomorphic/
